Both tools, verified:

```css
/* TRANSITION — state-change feedback: define the tween, change the property */
.card {
  transition: transform 200ms ease-out, opacity 200ms ease-out;
}
.card:hover { transform: translateY(-4px); }   /* old → new animated */

/* The 4 transition knobs: property, duration, timing-function, delay */
transition: transform 200ms cubic-bezier(0.2, 0, 0, 1) 100ms;

/* ANIMATION — a self-running timeline with keyframes */
@keyframes toast-in {
  0%   { transform: translateY(16px); opacity: 0; }
  60%  { transform: translateY(-2px); opacity: 1; }
  100% { transform: translateY(0);    opacity: 1; }
}
.toast {
  animation: toast-in 300ms ease-out both;   /* 'both' = fill forwards AND back */
}
/* animation-only knobs: iteration-count, direction (alternate),
   fill-mode, play-state (paused — JS toggles without resetting) */
```

The performance law (say it before writing any animation): transform and opacity are COMPOSITOR-only — the browser moves/merges an already-painted layer; layout properties (top/left/width/height/margin) re-run LAYOUT for every frame, and paint properties (background/color) repaint. At 60fps the budget is 16.7ms/frame — layout eats it instantly. will-change: transform pre-promotes a layer when you KNOW something will animate (but it costs memory — use for the animating elements, not the page).

The choosing rule: feedback tied to a STATE (hover, focus, class toggle, open/closed) → transition (declare once, the state machine drives it); a TIMELINE (looping indicator, multi-stop entrance, sequence with delays) → animation. For JS-orchestrated (element.animate() — the WAAPI) you get keyframe power with Promise semantics: finished resolves, and you can cancel; the modern middle ground between CSS and canvas/Web Animations for imperative motion.
